From: picture-frame build
Subject: [PATCH] drm/rockchip: rotate the output with the RK3128 RGA

Panels that are mounted rotated need every frame rotated, and the VOP of
the RK3126/RK3128 cannot rotate. With "rockchip,output-rotation" (90 or
270 degrees clockwise) and "rockchip,rga" in the VOP node and
rockchipdrm.rga_rotation=1, the connector reports rotated modes, the
primary plane takes framebuffers in that orientation (and is the only
plane), and the RGA (version 1, new driver rockchip_rga1.c) rotates the
damaged part of every new framebuffer into one of two buffers in the
orientation of the panel, in the commit tail. Compositors can then scan
out client buffers directly instead of rotating them on the GPU.

The register values for the RGA follow the Rockchip vendor driver
(drivers/video/rockchip/rga). /sys/kernel/debug/rockchip-rga1 shows the
job statistics.

Measured on the Denver frame (1280x800, Chromium in cage): a full frame
takes the RGA 8-10 ms on an idle system and about 20 ms while Chromium
renders, which limits a full-screen crossfade to 28 fps (34 fps with
the rotation on the GPU). Off by default for that reason.
